update public.posts set title='Spiti on Two Wheels — A Journey of a Lifetime',category='Ride story',excerpt='What the high road teaches about preparation, pace and asking locals before pressing on.',body='Spiti rewards patience more than speed. The day should begin with weather, fuel and road-condition checks—not a distance target. Keep margin for landslides, water crossings and the way altitude changes concentration.

Pack light but do not improvise on hydration, warm layers, basic tools or puncture support. Share the day’s destination with someone off the ride, and treat a local warning as useful intelligence rather than an obstacle to the plan.

The best memory is rarely the fastest section. It is usually the tea stop where another rider shares a road update, or the decision to turn back early enough to ride again tomorrow.',author_name='Aarav Mehta',sources='[]'::jsonb,hero_image_url='/images/community-post-1.jpg' where slug='spiti-on-two-wheels';
update public.posts set title='Chain Maintenance Guide for Long Tours',category='Maintenance',excerpt='A simple inspection rhythm that prevents a neglected chain from defining your tour.',body='Use the motorcycle and chain manufacturer’s instructions as the authority for cleaning products, lubrication and slack. Before a tour, inspect the chain and sprockets for tight spots, damaged links, hooked teeth or unusual noise.

Check slack with the motorcycle positioned exactly as the owner’s manual specifies. Too tight can load bearings and suspension movement; too loose can affect control and damage surrounding parts. If adjustment is unfamiliar, let a qualified technician do it.

After heavy rain, dust or a long day, inspect again. Cleaning should be gentle, and lubricant needs time to settle before the next ride. Replacement is a system decision: a worn chain and worn sprockets are normally assessed together.',author_name='BikeHub Workshop Desk',sources='[]'::jsonb,hero_image_url='/images/community-post-2.jpg' where slug='chain-maintenance-long-tours';
update public.posts set title='Prepare Your Adventure Bike for the Long Road',category='Expert advice',excerpt='Turn preparation into a short repeatable system instead of a last-night rush.',body='Start several days before departure. Confirm the service schedule, tyre condition, brake feel, fluid levels, lights, fasteners and any luggage mounting points. A last-minute accessory installation is not a substitute for testing the loaded motorcycle.

Build the tool kit around what you know how to use. Add essential documentation, emergency contacts, hydration, weather protection and a first-aid kit. Distribute luggage evenly and remain within the motorcycle and luggage-system limits.

Take a short loaded test ride. Notice steering, braking, suspension movement and anything that shifts or rubs. The goal is not to make the motorcycle expedition-looking; it is to make every item secure, necessary and understood.',author_name='Vikram S.',sources='[]'::jsonb,hero_image_url='/images/community-post-3.jpg' where slug='prepare-your-adventure-bike';
update public.posts set title='Monsoon Riding: Stay Safe and Stay on the Road',category='Riding tips',excerpt='Visibility, smooth control and enough time matter more than maintaining your dry-road pace.',body='Rain reduces visibility and can change grip quickly. Slow down before the difficult section, increase following distance and make steering, throttle and braking inputs progressive. Painted markings, metal covers, pooled water and road repairs deserve extra space.

A clear visor and visible protective gear help, but they do not remove the need to adjust speed. If rain becomes too heavy to read the road surface or traffic behaviour, choose a safe place away from the carriageway and wait.

After the ride, dry protective gear properly and inspect the motorcycle. Pay attention to tyre damage, brakes, lights, chain condition and any water that has entered luggage or electrical accessories.',author_name='Team BikeHub',sources='[{"label":"Road-safety visibility guidance","url":"https://nhmodisha.gov.in/wp-content/uploads/2024/02/Resource-Book-MO.pdf"}]'::jsonb,hero_image_url='/images/rider-community-generated.jpg' where slug='monsoon-riding';
update public.posts set title='Tyre Pressure: Why the Cold Check Matters',category='Maintenance',excerpt='Pressure changes handling, braking feel and tyre life—so measure it consistently.',body='Use the pressure specified by the motorcycle manufacturer for your load and use. A cold reading gives a repeatable baseline: check before riding, after the motorcycle has been stationary, rather than releasing air from a warm tyre.

Make the gauge part of your touring kit and inspect the valve, cap, tread and overall wear at the same time. A recurring pressure loss needs investigation; repeatedly topping it up is not a diagnosis.

Michelin recommends regular checks and a cold check before a long journey. Track and off-road settings are specialist use cases and should not be carried blindly onto public roads.',author_name='BikeHub Technical Desk',sources='[{"label":"Michelin motorcycle tyre-pressure guide","url":"https://www.michelin.in/motorbike/advice-motorbike/tyre-care/motorcycle-tyre-pressure-guide"}]'::jsonb,hero_image_url='/images/blog-tyre-pressure.jpg' where slug='motorcycle-tyre-pressure-cold-check';
update public.posts set title='The Five-Minute Pre-Ride Check',category='Riding tips',excerpt='A quick walk-around can reveal the small problem that would become a large one on the road.',body='Look at tyres and wheels, operate the controls, check lights and electrics, review fluid levels and leaks, inspect the chassis and confirm both stands return properly. This follows the logic of the established T-CLOCS inspection method.

The check is not a service replacement. Its value is comparison: when you know what normal looks and feels like, a loose fastener, soft control, fresh leak or damaged tyre becomes easier to notice.

If a safety-critical item feels wrong, pause the ride and get qualified help. The schedule can change; the consequences of hoping a fault disappears may not.',author_name='BikeHub Safety Desk',sources='[{"label":"Motorcycle Safety Foundation quick tips","url":"https://msf-usa.org/quick-tips/"}]'::jsonb,hero_image_url='/images/blog-preride-check.jpg' where slug='five-minute-pre-ride-check';
update public.posts set title='A Better Group Ride Starts Before Engines Start',category='Community',excerpt='Agree on pace, stops and what happens when the group separates before anyone rolls away.',body='Hold a short rider briefing. Confirm the route, fuel and rest stops, communication method, lead rider and sweep rider. The plan should reflect the least experienced rider, not the fastest motorcycle.

Keep groups manageable and use a staggered formation only where conditions allow. On narrow, curving, low-visibility or poor-surface roads, move to single file and increase the space available to react.

If riders separate, follow the regrouping plan instead of speeding to catch up. A good group ride removes pressure, makes intentions predictable and helps each person finish with energy in reserve.',author_name='BikeHub Ride Council',sources='[{"label":"Motorcycle Safety Foundation group-riding guide","url":"https://msf-usa.org/documents/group-riding/"}]'::jsonb,hero_image_url='/images/blog-group-riding.jpg' where slug='group-ride-briefing';
update public.posts set title='A Touring Kit That Earns Its Space',category='Touring',excerpt='Pack for likely problems, your actual skills and the conditions—not for photographs.',body='Start with essentials: documents, hydration, weather protection, first aid, communication and a compact tool or repair kit suited to the motorcycle. Carrying a tool you have never used is less valuable than knowing where qualified help is available.

Keep frequently needed items accessible and secure every load. Heavy items belong low and close to the motorcycle’s centre. Recheck straps and mounting points after the first stop.

For group travel, coordinate shared equipment instead of duplicating everything. At least one first-aid kit, a suitable tool kit and working phones are sensible foundations, alongside a clear assistance plan.',author_name='BikeHub Route Desk',sources='[{"label":"Motorcycle Safety Foundation group-riding preparation","url":"https://msf-usa.org/documents/group-riding/"}]'::jsonb,hero_image_url='/images/blog-touring-kit.jpg' where slug='touring-kit-that-earns-its-space';
